      "content": "I strongly advise against giving your child salt tablets, as they are not formulated for children and can lead to toxicity if misused. It's critical that any attempt to remedy this at home does not put your child at greater risk.\n\nIf he continues to resist fluids, it may also be helpful to try a different approach. Try offering oral rehydration solutions more suited to children's needs, or even pediatric-friendly electrolyte drinks available at the store. These are designed to help with rehydration and can sometimes be easier for a child to drink.\n\nSince hydration here is likely more urgent, given his refusal to drink and noticeable signs of dehydration, it is important to contact a healthcare professional as soon as possible. You could reach out to a pediatrician or use a telehealth service to get immediate advice. If an ER visit seems necessary for his current symptoms—excessive sleepiness, dry mouth, and got refusal to drink—you should not delay in taking him to urgent care to get professional help.",
